title: ARTEX / Exposed Secrets
page: breaches/artex-github-secrets-azure.html
as-of: 2026-11-21
promise: Microsoft links GitHub scanning for secrets to ARTEX; in a follow-on attack, app credentials of unresolved origin reached Key Vault, SharePoint and MSPs' clients, per Microsoft and Todyl.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: Dozens | apps with successful service principal sign-ins | across multiple tenants, per Microsoft
stat: 33+ | of about 45 targeted apps authenticated | in Todyl's telemetry, at about 18 organizations
stat: 27 | organizations reached through those apps | including MSPs' clients, per Todyl, which calls it a floor
Microsoft names no actor. Todyl assesses the activity as unrelated to the Storm-0940 ConnectWise compromise.
notes:
Start with the three figures the page leads with, and with who stands behind each one.

Microsoft reports successful service principal sign-ins for dozens of applications across multiple tenants. The page gives no exact count.

Todyl, whose team monitors its managed service provider (MSP) partners, counted about 45 targeted app registrations at about 18 organizations by November 3, and more than 33 of them authenticated. It says the actor reached 27 organizations through those apps, including MSPs' downstream clients. Todyl's figures cover only organizations that send it logs, and it calls them a floor.

Keep each figure with its source when you say it aloud.

Microsoft names no actor, and this deck names none. Todyl assesses the activity as unrelated to the ConnectWise Cloud Backup compromise attributed to Storm-0940.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: August 2026 | Microsoft starts observing GitHub queries for secrets
event: October 28 | Microsoft's earliest reported successful sign-ins
event: November 1 | Todyl first sees the main address
event: November 3 | Detections fire; Todyl sees every token replay blocked
event: November 5 | Microsoft publishes its account
event: November 6 | Todyl's update calls it AI-driven tooling
Microsoft names no actor. Where the credentials came from is unresolved. The page gives no end date. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
Every date on this timeline is one the page gives.

From August 2026, Microsoft says, an agentic AI offensive framework submitted large volumes of GitHub queries for secrets. Microsoft then describes a follow-on attack beginning as early as October 28, in which valid application credentials were used to sign in as service principals across multiple tenants.

Todyl first saw the main address at 08:45 UTC on November 1, four days after Microsoft's earliest successes. On November 3 detections fired, and Conditional Access blocked every refresh token replay Todyl saw. Microsoft Threat Intelligence and Microsoft Security Research published their account on November 5. Todyl's November 6 update called the activity AI-driven tooling acting with little human direction.

Where the credentials came from is unresolved, and the next slides take that up, along with the other caveats the page sets next to the headline.

The markers are evenly spaced, and the gaps between them are not to scale.

== Where the credentials came from is unresolved
layout: compare
col: Todyl, November 3 | Origin unknown | Read secret pairs as pointing to a file store or live backend | A tampered Claude Code extension: plausible, tied to no affected organization
col: Todyl, November 6 | An agentic credential crawler | Read Microsoft's GitHub finding as confirmation | Does not address its November 3 evidence
Microsoft leans toward GitHub but, for sign-ins from October 28, says only leaked or stolen. Todyl's reading goes further than Microsoft does.
notes:
This is the first caveat the page puts next to the headline: where the credentials came from is unresolved, and Todyl's own updates lean two ways.

On November 3 Todyl wrote that the origin was unknown. Several seed secrets had only ever been used from staff devices and office networks, and some arrived as pairs of a current and an expired secret, which it read as pointing to a file store or live backend and away from configurations crawled from the internet. The same day it listed a tampered Claude Code extension in Cursor's marketplace as a plausible source, without tying any affected organization to an install.

On November 6 it called the root cause an agentic credential crawler, citing AbuseIPDB reports of credential scanning from the actor's address before the first sign-in, no sign of prior compromise, and custom-built apps. That update does not address the November 3 evidence.

Microsoft leans toward GitHub without settling it for these sign-ins. For the sign-ins from October 28, it says only that the credentials were leaked or stolen.

== Agent or person? The accounts read it differently
layout: compare
col: Todyl, November 3 | Its telemetry did not show an AI model directing the activity | Read idle gaps and a switch to spoofed browser signatures as a person steering | Held AI direction at low confidence
col: Todyl, November 6 | AI-driven tooling acting with little human direction | Lists the switch to spoofed browser user agents as crawler behavior | Does not revisit the idle gaps or its earlier low confidence
Microsoft's published evidence for agents is speed and API retries, and nothing it publishes separates an agent from a script or an operator.
notes:
The second caveat is whether an agent or a person was driving.

Todyl's November 3 update held AI direction at low confidence, because its telemetry did not show a model directing the activity. It read multi-hour idle gaps and a switch to spoofed browser signatures as a person steering the tooling.

Its November 6 update reads the activity the other way. It calls it an agentic credential crawler, AI-driven tooling that acts with little human direction, and lists the switch to spoofed browser user agents as crawler behavior, without revisiting the idle gaps or its earlier low confidence.

Microsoft's published evidence for agents is speed and API retries. The page notes that the retries show a client correcting its own API errors within 28 to 80 seconds, and that Microsoft publishes nothing that separates an agent from a script or an operator.

When someone asks whether AI ran this, the page's answer is that Todyl's two updates read it differently, and that Microsoft's published evidence does not separate an agent from a script or an operator.

== Where ARTEX is named, and where it is not
layout: table
row: Where | What it says
row: Microsoft, summary | The actors used ARTEX to search GitHub for OAuth app secrets
row: Microsoft, body | An agentic AI offensive framework; ARTEX is an example of such tooling
row: Microsoft, sign-ins | Only "an agentic framework"
row: Microsoft, detections | Unattributed ARTEX-related network indicators, not published
row: Todyl, November 6 | Writes that Microsoft names the tooling as ARTEX
Todyl's claim goes beyond Microsoft's post. Microsoft does not say how it identified the framework, and it names no actor.
notes:
The third point is how firmly ARTEX is tied to this activity, and the answer depends on which part of Microsoft's post you read.

Microsoft's own summary says the actors used ARTEX to search GitHub for OAuth application secrets. The body is more guarded. It calls the tool an agentic AI offensive framework and offers ARTEX as an example of tooling that lets actors scan many repositories faster, then have agents analyze the output and carry out follow-on attacks. For the sign-ins from October 28, it says only that an agentic framework was used.

Its detection table refers to unattributed network indicators related to ARTEX use, and notes that suspected ARTEX use can extend beyond this GitHub scanning. It does not publish those indicators or say how it identified the framework.

Todyl's November 6 update writes that Microsoft names the tooling as ARTEX. The page says that goes beyond Microsoft's post.

Naming the tooling is not naming an actor. Microsoft names no actor here, and neither does this deck.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Reconnaissance | GitHub secret queries | T1593.003
step: 02 | Initial Access | Signing in as the workload | T1078.004
step: 03 | Credential Access | Resource Manager then Key Vault | T1555.006
step: 04 | Discovery | Mapping Azure | T1580
step: 05 | Collection | Files and mailbox items | T1213.002
step: 06 | Initial Access | From the MSP to its clients | T1199
step: 07 | Lateral Movement | Beyond app secrets | T1550.001
step: 08 | Impact | AI keys and the bill | T1496.004
Arrows show order only; the page leaves open where stage 02's credentials came from.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's eight stages, each with its phase and ATT&CK technique.

Stage 01 is reconnaissance, GitHub queries for secrets. Stage 02 is initial access, signing in as service principals. Stage 03 is credential access through Resource Manager and Key Vault. Stage 04 is discovery, mapping each Azure environment. Stage 05 is collection from SharePoint, OneDrive and mailboxes. Stage 06 is initial access again, this time from MSPs into their clients' tenants. Stage 07 is lateral movement beyond app secrets, and stage 08 is impact: AI keys and the bill.

Read the arrows as the page's stage order and nothing more. The page leaves open whether the GitHub queries in stage 01 supplied the credentials used in stage 02, and its stages combine Microsoft's and Todyl's accounts across multiple tenants. The next slides take each stage in turn.

== Large volumes of GitHub queries for secrets
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Reconnaissance
technique: T1593.003 | Search Open Websites/Domains: Code Repositories
fact: Since | August 2026, per Microsoft
fact: Query terms | target name, secret keywords, a cloud or AI provider keyword
fact: Sought | credentials, application credentials, AI inference API keys
fact: Tooling | an agentic framework that Microsoft links to ARTEX
Microsoft says removing a leaked secret does not invalidate it or erase its copies in edit history, caches, archives and logs.
notes:
Microsoft says that starting in August 2026 it observed threat actors using an agentic AI offensive framework to submit large volumes of queries to GitHub for secrets. The page maps this to T1593.003, Search Open Websites/Domains: Code Repositories.

The queries often included a target organization's name, keywords for secrets, or a keyword tied to cloud services or to another AI service provider. The secrets sought included credentials, application credentials and API keys for AI inference. Microsoft does not quantify the volume.

Microsoft links this scanning to ARTEX. By default, Microsoft says, ARTEX uses existing tools such as Nmap, curl, wget, Python HTTP clients and headless Chromium through Playwright.

The line at the bottom comes from the page's defenses. Microsoft is explicit that removing a secret does not invalidate it or erase the copies in edit history, caches, archives and logs, and that you should revoke or rotate it and investigate its historical use.

== Signing in as service principals
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1078.004 | Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts
fact: Window | as early as October 28 (Microsoft); November 1 to 3 (Todyl)
fact: Microsoft | successful sign-ins on dozens of apps across multiple tenants
fact: Targeted (Todyl) | about 45 apps at about 18 organizations; 33+ authenticated
fact: Credential origin | unresolved
An app sign-in raises no MFA prompt, and Conditional Access scoped to users does not apply to it, as Todyl points out.
notes:
Microsoft describes a follow-on attack, beginning as early as October 28, in which valid leaked or stolen application credentials reached Entra ID tenants and the Azure and Microsoft 365 services connected to them. The page maps it to T1078.004, Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts. Microsoft says the sign-ins came mainly from 199.19.77[.]84 and 199.19.77[.]18, a Tor exit node that may also carry unrelated malicious traffic.

By Todyl's November 3 count, about 45 app registrations at roughly 18 organizations were targeted and more than 33 authenticated, using client secrets and, in at least one case, a workload identity federated credential. A failed client-secret attempt and a successful one often appeared for the same app seconds to minutes apart. Scripted clients made 95% of sign-ins, in bursts of up to 24 in 10 seconds.

Todyl saw every confirmed app sign-in come from 199.19.77[.]84, and linked 199.19.77[.]18 to the operator only at low to moderate confidence, for Azure CLI password spraying and probing. It tied 65.87.223[.]26 to the same spraying from October 4 to 27, and says every attempt from the two spray addresses failed.

MFA is multi-factor authentication. The next slide explains service principals in general.

== What an app registration and a service principal are
layout: background
source: Microsoft Learn, application and service principal objects | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity-platform/app-objects-and-service-principals
Registering an app creates one application object, in its home tenant. A service principal is the app's instance in each tenant where it is used, and it defines what the app can do there.
notes:
Background, from Microsoft Learn, in general terms.

To reach resources secured by a Microsoft Entra tenant, an entity needs a security principal: a user principal for a person, a service principal for an application.

The application object is the global template, held in the tenant where the app was registered. A service principal is created in every tenant where the application is used, and it sets what the app can do there, who can access it and what resources it can reach. A single-tenant application has one service principal, in its home tenant. A multitenant application also gets one in each tenant where a user has consented to its use.

Microsoft lists three types of service principal: application, managed identity and legacy. Managed identities remove the need for developers to manage credentials, and a managed identity's service principal has no app object. An app registration, by contrast, can hold secrets or certificates.

== Tokens for Resource Manager, then Key Vault
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Credential Access
technique: T1555.006 | Credentials from Password Stores: Cloud Secrets Management Stores
fact: Token order (Todyl) | Resource Manager, then Key Vault, often then Graph and others
fact: Microsoft | targeted storage keys, connection strings, AKS user credentials
fact: Connector (Todyl) | archive downloaded, its app signed in about 2.5 minutes later
fact: Microsoft, one case | GitHub search to Key Vault exfiltration in five minutes
Previously unused apps authenticated within minutes. Todyl says the timing suggests Key Vault reads were one source; Key Vault audit logs are needed to confirm it.
notes:
Todyl saw the same order for nearly every credential: an Azure Resource Manager token, then a Key Vault token, often followed within seconds by Graph, Storage and Cognitive Services tokens. The page maps this stage to T1555.006, Cloud Secrets Management Stores.

Within minutes, apps that had never been used before started authenticating. Todyl says the timing suggests Key Vault reads were one source of that new material, and that Key Vault audit logs are needed to confirm it. In one case a connector archive was downloaded and the actor signed in as that connector's app about 2.5 minutes later.

Microsoft says the actor targeted operations that return credentials: storage account keys, App Service settings, connection strings and publishing credentials, AI and machine learning connections and datastores, and AKS user credentials. AKS is Azure Kubernetes Service. In some cases, Microsoft says, the agents exfiltrated secrets from the organization's Key Vault.

Microsoft also reports that in at least one case an AI agent went from searching GitHub for client secrets to exfiltrating Key Vault data in five minutes. Keep the hedges: it is one case, Microsoft publishes no step timings or victim, and it does not say outright that the case was part of the activity from October 28.

== Mapping Azure, and correcting its own calls
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Discovery
technique: T1580 | Cloud Infrastructure Discovery
fact: Mapped (Microsoft) | subscriptions, resource groups, permissions, AI deployments
fact: Key Vault metadata | HTTP 400; about 28 s later, API version 2023-07-01, HTTP 200
fact: SQL firewall rules | HTTP 400; about 60 s later, only api-version changed, HTTP 200
fact: Third example | an Application Insights query, about 80 s apart
With the five-minute case, these are Microsoft's most specific evidence for agents; it publishes nothing that separates an agent from a script or an operator.
notes:
Microsoft says the actor enumerated subscriptions, resource groups, permissions, AI model deployments and service configurations to map each environment. The page maps this to T1580, Cloud Infrastructure Discovery.

Against one victim's Resource Manager, from 199.19.77[.]84, Microsoft gives three examples of a failed request followed by a corrected one. A batch enumerating Key Vault secret metadata used a data-plane API version against the control plane and returned HTTP 400, and about 28 seconds later a rerun against the same paths with API version 2023-07-01 returned 200. A request for an Azure SQL server's firewall rules failed on an outdated API version, and a matching request about 60 seconds later succeeded with only the api-version parameter changed. An Application Insights query followed the same pattern about 80 seconds apart.

Microsoft calls these sequences consistent with adaptive troubleshooting. Keep the page's reading beside it: they show a client correcting its own API errors, and Microsoft publishes nothing that separates an agent from a script or an operator.

The next slide explains the control plane and the data plane in general.

== Azure's control plane and data plane
layout: background
source: Microsoft Learn, Azure control plane and data plane | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/management/control-plane-and-data-plane
credit: Also from the Azure REST API reference, and Secure your Azure Key Vault (learn.microsoft.com)
The control plane manages resources in a subscription; every request goes to Azure Resource Manager, whose APIs require an api-version parameter. The data plane uses what a resource instance exposes, like a storage account's data, at its own endpoint.
notes:
Background, from Microsoft Learn, in general terms.

Azure divides operations into the control plane and the data plane. You create a storage account through the control plane, then read and write its data through the data plane.

Every control plane request goes to the Azure Resource Manager URL, which for Azure global is management.azure.com. Resource Manager applies Azure role-based access control, Azure Policy, management locks and activity logs to those requests. Data plane requests go to an endpoint specific to the resource instance. Microsoft notes that features enforcing management and governance might not apply to data plane operations: a lock that prevents deleting a database does not stop someone deleting data through queries.

Key Vault has both interfaces. Its control plane manages Key Vault itself, and its data plane works with keys, secrets and certificates.

The Azure REST API reference says Resource Manager provider APIs require an api-version query-string parameter: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/azure/

And from Secure your Azure Key Vault: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/key-vault/general/secure-key-vault

== Files named pass, key and access
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Collection
technique: T1213.002 | Data from Information Repositories: Sharepoint
fact: Searches (Todyl) | 29 wildcard Graph searches across about 25 tenants
fact: Downloads (Todyl) | 81 files at about one per second, favoring such names
fact: Mail items (Todyl) | about 2,279 accessed
fact: Not seen (Todyl) | extortion demand, encryption or destruction
Per Todyl, the files held password lists, private keys and VPN profiles. It expects their use against systems it cannot see from Microsoft 365 logs.
notes:
Microsoft says the compromised applications accessed SharePoint and OneDrive, including file downloads and mailbox-item access, and that the actor's Microsoft Graph requests were mostly searches for content of interest. The page maps this to T1213.002, Data from Information Repositories: Sharepoint.

Todyl counted 29 single-wildcard Graph searches across about 25 tenants, followed by bulk SharePoint downloads at about one per second that favored file names such as pass, key and access. Its November 3 totals were 81 files downloaded and about 2,279 mailbox items accessed.

Todyl lists what the collected files held: password lists, private keys, VPN profiles, MFA and RMM API keys, backup-storage credentials, firewall and RDP (Remote Desktop Protocol) configurations, Azure environment inventories and service-account maps. RMM is remote monitoring and management. Todyl expects the files to be used against VPNs, MFA services, RMM and monitoring platforms, backup storage and network devices, exposure it cannot see from Microsoft 365 logs.

Todyl saw no extortion demand, encryption or destruction, and says data was accessed.

== MSP apps carried access into client tenants
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1199 | Trusted Relationship
fact: One app | 15 client tenants in about 10-second loops (Todyl)
fact: Partner Center | a token obtained by one MSP's app (Todyl)
fact: GDAP | automation app tried against clients, without success (Todyl)
fact: Vendor apps | a multitenant credential compromised at the source (Microsoft)
Security tools' integration apps were among those abused, including Todyl's own in two MSP tenants. Todyl says there is no sign any vendor platform was compromised.
notes:
Todyl says the actor reached 27 organizations through the apps it signed in as, including MSPs' downstream clients in government, energy, legal, education, nonprofits and other sectors. The page maps this to T1199, Trusted Relationship.

On November 3 a single multi-tenant app swept 15 client tenants in loops of about 10 seconds. One MSP's app obtained a Partner Center token, and the same MSP's GDAP automation app was tried against client tenants without success.

Integration apps for security tools were among those abused, including Todyl's own integration apps in two MSP tenants, and Todyl says there is no sign any vendor platform was compromised.

Microsoft describes the same shape from the vendor side: cases where a multitenant application credential was compromised at the source, a vendor-owned application, and used to access multiple downstream tenants. It does not name the vendor.

The next slide explains GDAP in general.

== What GDAP gives a Microsoft partner
layout: background
source: Microsoft Learn, introduction to GDAP | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/partner-center/customers/gdap-introduction
credit: Also from GDAP frequently asked questions (learn.microsoft.com)
Granular delegated admin privileges (GDAP), set up through Partner Center, give a Microsoft partner granular, time-bound access to a customer's workloads. The customer must explicitly grant that least-privileged access.
notes:
Background, from Microsoft Learn's Partner Center documentation, in general terms.

Microsoft describes GDAP as a security feature that gives partners least-privileged access, following Zero Trust. Partners configure granular, time-bound access to customers' workloads in production and sandbox environments, customers must explicitly grant it, and access can be partitioned per customer.

Partners who manage Azure no longer receive the Global Admin role on a customer's tenant. By default they receive lower permissions to read the customer directory.

Microsoft's FAQ adds that a GDAP relationship cannot be permanent. Its maximum duration is two years, and auto extend, when enabled, extends it by six months at a time until it is terminated or auto extend is disabled. Someone with the Admin agent role at a partner organization can request a relationship.

Also from GDAP frequently asked questions: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/partner-center/customers/gdap-faq

== Azure DevOps, replayed tokens and Run Command
layout: stage
stage: 07
phase: Lateral Movement
technique: T1550.001 | Use Alternate Authentication Material: Application Access Token
fact: Azure DevOps | federated identity used; two users' sessions replayed (Todyl)
fact: VMs | Run Command called through a compromised OAuth app (Microsoft)
fact: Refresh tokens | replayed November 3, all blocked by Conditional Access (Todyl)
fact: Persistence | none seen by Todyl
Todyl warns that rotation is not enough on its own: the actor kept retrying disabled and rotated apps and moved on to replaying users' refresh tokens.
notes:
Microsoft says the agents searched Azure DevOps for secrets and used a compromised OAuth application to call Run Command, which runs scripts on Azure VMs. It does not say what ran. The page maps this stage to T1550.001, Application Access Token.

Todyl saw an Azure DevOps federated workload identity used from the actor's address from 14:39 UTC on November 1, and two users' Azure DevOps sessions replayed on November 2, the first at 10:39 UTC. Reported use of personal access tokens, or PATs, was still being examined.

After detections fired at 07:57 UTC on November 3, the actor switched from curl and Python to spoofed browser user agents. Todyl's last recorded success came at 15:16 UTC. By 18:12 UTC the actor was replaying several users' refresh tokens with scripted clients, and Conditional Access blocked every replay, including its latest attempt at 21:31 UTC.

It kept retrying credentials against disabled and rotated apps, and Todyl saw no persistence: no new consents, credentials, roles or users.

The next slide explains workload identity federation in general.

== What workload identity federation does
layout: background
source: Microsoft Learn, workload identity federation concepts | https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/workload-id/workload-identity-federation
Workload identity federation lets an app registration or user-assigned managed identity trust tokens from an external identity provider. The workload trades that provider's token for a Microsoft access token, with no secret to manage.
notes:
Background, from Microsoft Learn, in general terms.

A workload running outside Azure, or one that uses an app registration for its identity, otherwise needs an application credential, a secret or a certificate, which has to be stored securely and rotated regularly. Federation replaces that with a trust relationship.

A federated identity credential on the app registration or managed identity says which external tokens to trust. Its issuer, subject and audience values must match the token's values, case included.

The workload gets a token from its identity provider and sends it to the Microsoft identity platform. The platform checks the trust relationship, validates the token against the provider's OpenID Connect issuer URL, and issues an access token, which the workload uses on Microsoft Entra protected resources.

Microsoft lists supported scenarios including GitHub Actions, Kubernetes clusters, workloads in other clouds, and Azure Resource Manager service connections in Azure Pipelines.

== AI service keys, and one partner's bill
layout: stage
stage: 08
phase: Impact
technique: T1496.004 | Resource Hijacking: Cloud Service Hijacking
fact: Focus (Microsoft) | AI provider API keys and apps with Foundry Tools access
fact: Tokens (Todyl) | four AI-service tokens at two MSPs, which do not prove use
fact: Billing (Todyl) | thousands of dollars in about 10 minutes, one partner
fact: Motive (Todyl) | fits an initial access broker or LLM access resale
Microsoft says the actor attempted to validate stolen Azure resources, including compromised Azure AI services, and might have attempted to abuse some of them.
notes:
Microsoft says the agents repeatedly focused on credentials for AI services, such as API keys for major AI providers and OAuth applications with access to Foundry Tools, formerly Azure Cognitive Services. The page maps this to T1496.004, Resource Hijacking: Cloud Service Hijacking.

The actor attempted to validate stolen Azure resources, including compromised Azure AI services, and Microsoft says it might have attempted to abuse some of them. Separately, Microsoft observed reconnaissance of AI workloads on Azure AI services from 157.22.126[.]128.

Todyl saw four AI-service tokens issued at two MSPs, three for Cognitive Services and one for Azure Machine Learning, and notes that an issued token does not prove use. Its November 6 update adds that one partner was billed thousands of dollars of API usage in about 10 minutes.

Todyl says the activity fits either an initial access broker or the resale of LLM API access. LLM stands for large language model.

The next slide explains what ATT&CK means by cloud service hijacking, in general.

== What ATT&CK calls Cloud Service Hijacking
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1496.004 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1496/004/
Adversaries may use compromised software-as-a-service applications for resource-intensive tasks, such as mass spam or, in LLMjacking, hijacking cloud-hosted AI models. The victim may face significant costs, used-up service quotas and other effects on availability.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

Cloud Service Hijacking is a sub-technique of Resource Hijacking, under the Impact tactic. ATT&CK's examples include email and messaging services such as AWS Simple Email Service, Simple Notification Service, SendGrid and Twilio, used to send large volumes of spam and phishing, and LLMjacking, which uses reverse proxies to hijack the power of cloud-hosted AI models.

Adversaries may use services the victim already runs, or, particularly when the service is part of a larger cloud platform, enable it first. The result can be significant financial cost, used-up service quotas and other effects on availability.

ATT&CK says this technique cannot be easily mitigated with preventive controls, because it abuses system features. Its detection strategy looks for access to services such as AWS SES, SNS or the OpenAI API, changes to usage policies, and resource-intensive actions, often from unauthorized regions or under anomalous identity conditions.

== Six defenses against eight stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05 | 06 | 07 | 08
row: Rotate secrets that reached a public repository | 01
row: Contain the app in Microsoft's order | 02 | 03 | 05 | 07 | 08
row: Conditional Access for workload identities, plus a user block | 02 | 07
row: Hunt back to at least October 28 | 02 | 03
row: Retire client secrets, protect the issuer, fence Key Vault and Storage | 02 | 03 | 07
row: MSPs audit partner apps and GDAP | 06
No defense on the page is tied to stage 04, the discovery and API retries.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's six defenses against the eight stages. A cell is marked only where the defense's own text refers to that stage's activity.

Rotating secrets that reached a public repository answers stage 01, whose GitHub queries often named a target organization. It is not marked for stage 02, because the page leaves open where those credentials came from.

Containing the app in Microsoft's order removes the client secrets and federated credentials used in stage 02, rotates what the Key Vaults of stage 03 and the documents of stage 05 held, revokes the user sessions stage 07 replayed and Azure DevOps PATs, whose reported use the page says was still being examined, and, per Todyl, rotates AI service keys and reviews billing for stage 08.

A Conditional Access policy for workload identities targets the app sign-ins of stage 02, within the limits the page lists, and a user-scoped block covers the replayed sessions of stage 07. Hunting covers the sign-ins of stage 02 and the token sequence of stage 03. Retiring client secrets answers stage 02, fencing Key Vault and Storage answers stage 03, and protecting the federation issuer, here Azure DevOps, answers stage 07. The MSP audit answers stage 06.

No defense on the page is tied to stage 04; that gap is a discussion question.

== Rotate exposed secrets, and contain the app
layout: compare
col: Rotate exposed secrets | Treat a secret in a public repository as compromised | Removing it does not invalidate it, per Microsoft | Secret scanning with public monitoring, and push protection | Todyl: stop AI agents using or posting cleartext secrets
col: Contain the app | Remove every client secret, certificate and federated credential | Disable it if needed; issued tokens stay valid until expiry | Rotate Key Vaults it could read, and credentials in documents it accessed | Todyl adds: rotate AI keys, review billing
Microsoft also lists revoking users' Entra sessions, Azure DevOps PATs and SSH keys.
notes:
Two defenses come first on the page.

Treat any secret that has reached a public repository as compromised, and rotate it now. Microsoft is explicit that removing a secret does not invalidate it or erase the copies in edit history, caches, archives and logs, and that you should revoke or rotate it and investigate its historical use. GitHub secret scanning with public monitoring follows your secrets into other people's repositories, and push protection blocks supported secrets before they are pushed. Todyl's first recommendation is guardrails that stop AI agents from using or posting secrets in cleartext on the command line, which it calls the largest contributor to exposed credentials it has observed.

Then contain an affected app in Microsoft's order. Remove every client secret, certificate and federated credential on the app, including any you did not add, and disable the service principal if needed. Tokens already issued stay valid until they expire. Then rotate everything in every Key Vault the app could read, and any credentials found in documents it accessed. For affected users, Microsoft lists revoking Entra sessions, Azure DevOps PATs and SSH keys. Todyl adds rotating AI service keys held in affected apps or vaults, reviewing their billing, and finding where the original credential was stored and who had it.

== Block app sign-ins with a policy for workload identities
layout: compare
col: What to do | Policies scoped to users miss app sign-ins; target workload identities | Microsoft: block 199.19.77[.]84/32, plus a user-scoped block for replays | Todyl: require a compliant device or trusted location
col: Its limits | Needs Workload ID Premium; single-tenant apps in your tenant only | Not managed identities, multitenant apps or third-party SaaS (Todyl) | The app that swept 15 client tenants was multi-tenant
The sources differ on scope: Microsoft says block the exact IP, not the ASN; Todyl's November 6 update recommends 199.19.77[.]0/24 and 65.87.223[.]26.
notes:
Block with a Conditional Access policy that targets workload identities, and do not rely on rotation alone. A policy scoped to users does not stop app sign-ins.

Microsoft's containment step puts 199.19.77[.]84/32 in a named location blocked by a workload identity policy assigned directly to service principals, plus a user-scoped block for replayed sessions.

Know the limits. Workload identity policies need Workload ID Premium and cover only single-tenant apps registered in your tenant, so, as Todyl spells out, not managed identities, multitenant apps or third-party SaaS. The app that swept 15 client tenants was multi-tenant.

Todyl warns that rotation is not enough on its own, because the actor kept retrying disabled and rotated apps and moved on to replaying users' refresh tokens. Conditional Access blocked every replay Todyl saw on November 3, and Todyl recommends policies that require a compliant or hybrid-joined device or limit sign-ins to trusted locations.

The sources differ on scope. Microsoft says to block the exact IP rather than the ASN, the autonomous system number for the whole network. Todyl's November 3 post said the same, and its November 6 update recommends blocking 199.19.77[.]0/24 along with 65.87.223[.]26.

== Retire client secrets; MSPs audit partner apps and GDAP
layout: compare
col: Retire client secrets | Managed identities first, then federation, then certificates (Todyl) | Todyl: app policies that block new secrets and cap lifetimes | Todyl: federation trusts the issuer, here Azure DevOps; protect it | Key Vault and Storage on selected networks or private endpoints
col: If you are an MSP | Review Partner Center access, GDAP and customer tenants | Notify affected customers under your agreements | Todyl: least-privilege, time-bound GDAP | Todyl: no customer app secrets in runbooks, notes or client documents
notes:
Two more defenses.

Retire client secrets, and keep Key Vault and Storage off the open internet. Microsoft and Todyl both recommend managed identities in place of secrets in code. Todyl ranks managed identities first, then workload identity federation, then certificates held in a vault, and suggests app management policies that block new secrets and cap credential lifetimes. Federation moves trust to the token issuer, here Azure DevOps, so Todyl says to protect that system as carefully. It also recommends restricting Key Vault and Storage to selected networks or private endpoints, so a valid token presented from an unknown address cannot reach the data. Microsoft adds least privilege for every service principal's Azure roles.

If you are an MSP, audit partner-tenant apps and GDAP, and tell your clients. Microsoft and Todyl both say to review Partner Center access, GDAP relationships and customer tenants, and to notify affected customers under your agreements. Todyl adds moving to least-privilege, time-bound GDAP and never storing customer app secrets in runbooks, PSA or RMM notes, or client documentation. PSA is professional services automation, and RMM is remote monitoring and management.

== Addresses to hunt, and what each source says
layout: table
row: Address | Microsoft | Todyl
row: 199.19.77[.]84 | a main source of sign-ins | every confirmed sign-in, download, replay
row: 199.19.77[.]18 | a main source; Tor exit node | spraying, probing; low to moderate confidence
row: 65.87.223[.]26 | potentially associated | same spraying, October 4 to 27; all failed
row: 157.22.126[.]128 | AI workload reconnaissance | -
row: Three Microsoft IPs | - | the actor's searches and mail reads appear under them; do not block
Both sources expect the infrastructure to change; hunt the behavior too.
notes:
The page names these addresses, and the sources do not always agree about them.

Microsoft says the sign-ins came mainly from 199.19.77[.]84 and 199.19.77[.]18, and notes that the second is a Tor exit node that may also carry unrelated malicious traffic. Todyl saw every confirmed app sign-in, download and token replay come from 199.19.77[.]84, and linked 199.19.77[.]18 to the operator only at low to moderate confidence, for Azure CLI password spraying and probing.

Todyl tied 65.87.223[.]26 to the same spraying from October 4 to 27, and says every attempt from the two spray addresses failed. Microsoft calls that address potentially associated with this campaign, and reports shell commands embedded in a user agent and attempts to obtain App Service authentication tokens from it. Microsoft also observed reconnaissance of AI workloads from 157.22.126[.]128.

Todyl notes that the actor's searches and mail reads are logged under three Microsoft service addresses, which are not actor infrastructure and should not be blocked. A dash in the table means the page reports nothing from that source.

== Hunt the behavior, and count the failures
layout: compare
col: Sign-in logs | Service principal and non-interactive sign-ins, back to at least October 28 | Include failures, as far back as retention allows | AADSTS7000215 or AADSTS7000222: someone holds your app's credential | A failure followed by a success is urgent
col: Behavior | One address, three or more service principals, mixed success | One app: Resource Manager, Key Vault, Storage and Graph tokens in a short window | A Resource Manager token, then Key Vault, then unused apps signing in within minutes
Actor-unique client fingerprints (Todyl): task177-f2267/1.0, sait-tenant-deep/37346.
notes:
Hunt back to at least October 28, and count the failures. Search service principal and non-interactive sign-in logs for 199.19.77[.]84 and 199.19.77[.]18, including failed attempts, as far back as retention allows, and expect unrelated noise from the Tor exit node. Microsoft's hunting queries also search Azure control-plane and Storage activity for 65.87.223[.]26. Todyl's first sighting came four days after Microsoft's earliest successes.

AADSTS7000215 means an invalid secret and AADSTS7000222 an expired one. Either means someone holds a credential for your app, and a failure followed by a success is urgent.

Both sources expect the infrastructure to change, so hunt the behavior too: one address authenticating to three or more service principals with mixed success; one app requesting Resource Manager, Key Vault, Storage and Graph tokens in a short window; and a Resource Manager token, then a Key Vault token, then previously unused apps in the same tenant authenticating within minutes.

Todyl lists two client fingerprints unique to the actor, task177-f2267/1.0 and sait-tenant-deep/37346.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Was every secret that reached a public repository rotated, not only removed?
check: Do you use secret scanning with public monitoring, and push protection?
check: Does a Conditional Access policy target your workload identities, not only your users?
check: Would you notice failed client-secret sign-ins to your apps, followed by a success?
check: Which of your apps still use client secrets rather than managed identities or federation?
check: Can any address with a valid token reach your Key Vaults and Storage?
check: If you are an MSP, when did you last review GDAP relationships?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether any of your secrets has reached a public repository, and whether it was rotated rather than only removed, since removing a secret does not invalidate it. Ask whether secret scanning with public monitoring and push protection are on.

Ask whether a Conditional Access policy targets your workload identities, since a policy scoped to users does not stop app sign-ins, and whether anything would flag AADSTS7000215 or AADSTS7000222 failures followed by a success.

Ask which apps still hold client secrets rather than managed identities or federation, and whether Key Vault and Storage are restricted to selected networks or private endpoints.

If you are an MSP, ask when you last reviewed Partner Center access, GDAP relationships and customer tenants.

== You rotated the leaked secret. Are you done?
layout: question
Suppose one of your apps' client secrets leaked and you rotated it.

Using what the page reports, what could still go wrong, and what would you check next?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

The question comes from a warning on the page: rotation is not enough on its own.

Ask each person to name one specific thing they would check, in their own environment, after rotating a leaked app secret. Then show the answer slide, which gives what the page says.

== Rotation alone is not enough, per the page
layout: answer
The page gives three reasons.
- Issued tokens stay valid until they expire.
- Todyl: the actor retried disabled and rotated apps, then replayed users' refresh tokens.
- Target workload identities with Conditional Access; rotate what the app could read.
notes:
The page's answer has three parts.

First, tokens already issued stay valid until they expire, so rotating the secret does not end access that was already granted.

Second, Todyl warns that rotation is not enough on its own, because the actor kept retrying disabled and rotated apps and moved on to replaying users' refresh tokens. Conditional Access blocked every replay Todyl saw on November 3.

Third, the page's defenses go past the secret. Microsoft's containment order removes every credential on the app, including any you did not add, disables the service principal if needed, and rotates everything in every Key Vault the app could read. The page also says to block app sign-ins with a Conditional Access policy that targets workload identities.

Bring the group's checks back to these. Any check that does not appear here is the group's own idea, which is fine, and worth testing against the facts the page reports.

== What would you add for stage 04?
layout: question
No page defense is tied to stage 04, where a client retried failed Resource Manager calls with corrected API versions within 28 to 80 seconds.

What would you watch for, and what would it catch?
notes:
This question comes from the empty column in the defense matrix.

The matrix ties the page's defenses to every stage except stage 04, where the actor enumerated subscriptions, resource groups, permissions, AI model deployments and service configurations, and corrected failed API calls within 28 to 80 seconds.

Let the group propose ideas before you show the answer slide. Ask each proposal to name the detail from the page that it would have caught.

== The page ties no defense to stage 04
layout: answer
Any control you propose is your own suggestion. Test it against the reported details: HTTP 400 followed by HTTP 200 after a corrected API version, and enumeration of subscriptions, permissions and AI deployments.
notes:
Be clear that the page stops here. It ties no defense to stage 04, and this deck adds none of its own.

If the group proposes ideas, treat them as the group's, and test each one against the facts the page reports. Those facts are the enumeration of subscriptions, resource groups, permissions, AI model deployments and service configurations, and the three failed requests followed by corrected ones: a Key Vault metadata batch, an Azure SQL firewall rules request and an Application Insights query, each corrected within about 28 to 80 seconds.

Remind the group of the page's caution. These retries show a client correcting its own API errors, and Microsoft publishes nothing that separates an agent from a script or an operator. A proposal built on them should say what it would catch, whoever is at the keyboard.

